    "pthm-td-controlled-continuum-thermodynamic-limit": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix an integer spatial dimension $d\\geq 1$, a reference length $\\ell_0>0$, and a bounded measurable region $\\Lambda\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ with dimensionless volume $V=|\\Lambda|$. Its physical region is $\\ell_0\\Lambda$. A finite positional configuration of $N$ classical point particles in $\\Lambda$ is an unordered simple set $\\omega=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_N\\}\\subset\\Lambda$; simple means that no point is repeated. Particle number $N$ is a dimensionless nonnegative integer and the physical number density corresponding to $N/V$ is $N/(\\ell_0^dV)$.\n\nA pair potential is an even measurable function $\\varphi:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$, where $\\varphi(x)$ is the interaction energy in joules at physical displacement $\\ell_0x$ and evenness means $\\varphi(x)=\\varphi(-x)$. For a labelled representative of $\\omega$, define the positional pair energy\n$$H_N(\\omega)=\\sum_{1\\leq i<j\\leq N}\\varphi(x_i-x_j),\\qquad H_0=H_1=0.$$\nIf any pair is forbidden by the potential, its energy and hence $H_N$ are $+\\infty$. This model contains no kinetic energy, internal molecular coordinates, quantum exchange terms, or external field.\n\nThe interaction is stable if there is a constant $B\\geq0$, measured in joules, such that $H_N(\\omega)\\geq-BN$ for every finite simple configuration. It has a regular integrable tail if there are $R>0$ and a nonnegative nonincreasing measurable function $\\psi:[R,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty)$, measured in joules, such that $|\\varphi(x)|\\leq\\psi(|x|)$ for $|x|\\geq R$ and\n$$\\int_R^\\infty r^{d-1}\\psi(r)\\,dr<\\infty.$$\nA stable potential with this tail condition is the stable regularly tempered class used for the free-cube results on this page. Superstability and a positive divergent core are stronger, separate hypotheses and are not implied by stability.\n\nAt temperature $T>0$, write $\\beta=(k_BT)^{-1}$ in joules inverse. The activity $z>0$ is dimensionless relative to the reference intensity $\\ell_0^{-d}$. A free-wall finite-volume model contains only positions in $\\Lambda$ and uses $H_N$ above. A periodic model uses the declared periodic cell and sums its periodic-image pair interactions with each unordered pair counted once. These are distinct boundary preparations. The limits on this page are positional configurational limits and do not by themselves include a momentum integral or a full phase-space energy shell.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-td-retained-hard-core-zero-beta-preparation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For the pair potential $\\varphi$ of [[def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation]], let $B=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^d:\\varphi(x)=+\\infty\\}$. A finite configuration is hard-admissible if every pair displacement avoids $B$. For $z>0$, define the retained-admissibility zero-inverse-temperature weight of $\\omega$ to be\n$$w_0(\\omega)=z^{N(\\omega)}\\mathbf 1_{\\{H_{N(\\omega)}(\\omega)<\\infty\\}}.$$\nIts finite-volume partition sum, when finite, is $\\Xi_{\\Lambda}^{\\mathrm{ret},0}(z)=\\sum_{N\\geq0}\\int w_0(\\omega)\\,\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}(d\\omega)$, using [[post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure]]. This definition sets the weight of a forbidden configuration to zero; it does not assign a value to the undefined product $0\\cdot(+\\infty)$. When $B=\\varnothing$ the measure is the unrestricted Poisson activity model; for a nonempty hard core it is generally not Poisson. The convention is explicit model data and carries no statement about an arbitrary zero-width energy shell.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-td-equilibrium-ensemble-sampling",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "For a specified finite region and boundary preparation, take $T>0$, $\\beta=(k_BT)^{-1}$, and the positional energy in [[def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation]]. For each fixed $N$, specialize the measurable-model canonical preparation of [[post-td-canonical-sampling]] to the dimensionless positional configuration measure $\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}$; adopt the law\n$$P_{\\Lambda,N}^{\\mathrm{can}}(d\\omega)=Z_{\\Lambda,N}^{-1}e^{-\\beta H_N(\\omega)}\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}(d\\omega),\\qquad Z_{\\Lambda,N}=\\int e^{-\\beta H_N}\\,d\\mu_{\\Lambda,N},$$\nwhenever $0<Z_{\\Lambda,N}<\\infty$. Separately adopt the grand-canonical law at activity $z>0$ to have weights $z^{N(\\omega)}e^{-\\beta H_{N(\\omega)}(\\omega)}$ relative to the same indistinguishable positional measures; its normalizer is $\\Xi_\\Lambda(z,\\beta)=\\sum_{N\\geq0}z^NZ_{\\Lambda,N}$ when finite. Here $z$ is dimensionless relative to the reference intensity in [[post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure]], and $\\alpha=\\log z$.\n\nFor the controlled positional shell of [[def-td-controlled-fixed-n-energy-shell]], adopt the conditional measure $M_n$ only when its shell has positive finite reference measure. These are probability-law preparations used to define the model. They are not derived from Hamiltonian trajectories, and they do not assert that a laboratory apparatus reaches any of them. The inverse-temperature-zero hard-core continuation is governed separately by [[def-td-retained-hard-core-zero-beta-preparation]].\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "post-td-grand-variational-phase-uniqueness",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Fix the selected stable regularly tempered pair model, $T>0$, $\\beta=(k_BT)^{-1}$, and activity $z>0$. For the superstable branch, require an explicit decomposition $\\varphi=\\varphi_s+\\varphi_r$ where $\\varphi_s$ is stable and there is a $\\delta>0$ such that $\\varphi_r(x)\\geq\\delta\\mathbf 1_{\\{|x|\\leq\\delta\\}}$. This is an additional model hypothesis, not a consequence of stability alone.\n\nA probability law $P$ on locally finite simple configurations is stationary if it is invariant under every spatial translation. Let $P_{\\Lambda_L}$ and $Q_{\\Lambda_L}$ be its restriction and the unit-intensity Poisson reference of [[post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure]] to a centered cube $\\Lambda_L$. For probability measures $P_0,Q_0$, define $D(P_0\\|Q_0)=\\int \\log(dP_0/dQ_0)\\,dP_0$ if $P_0$ is absolutely continuous with respect to $Q_0$, and $D=+\\infty$ otherwise. Let $C=[-1/2,1/2)^d$ and define $\\rho(P)=P(N_C)$. On stationary laws for which the finite limits exist, define\n$$I(P)=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}\\frac{D(P_{\\Lambda_L}\\|Q_{\\Lambda_L})}{|\\Lambda_L|},\\qquad \\Phi(P)=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}\\frac{P(H_{\\Lambda_L}^{\\mathrm{free}})}{|\\Lambda_L|}.$$\nFor $\\beta>0$, use the stationary laws with finite $I(P)$ and $\\Phi(P)$ and adopt, at the selected $(\\varphi,\\beta,z)$, that\n$$\\operatorname*{arg\\,min}_{P}\\{I(P)+\\beta\\Phi(P)-\\rho(P)\\log z\\}=\\{P^\\ast\\}.$$\n\nFor the $\\beta=0$ branch, require a nonempty hard-core displacement set $B=\\{x:\\varphi(x)=+\\infty\\}$ and use [[def-td-retained-hard-core-zero-beta-preparation]]. Restrict to stationary laws supported on hard-admissible configurations with finite $I(P)$ and adopt, at the selected $z$, that\n$$\\operatorname*{arg\\,min}_{P}\\{I(P)-\\rho(P)\\log z\\}=\\{P^\\ast\\}.$$\nThese are two explicit parameter-specific physical model restrictions. They do not assert uniqueness for every activity or temperature, do not apply at coexistence when the minimizer set has several elements, and do not infer uniqueness from thermodynamic smoothness.\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L0",
          "source": "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Fix an integer dimension $d\\geq1$, use the Euclidean norm $|\\cdot|_2$ from [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], define $|x|_\\infty=\\max_j|x_j|$, and use the numerical coordinate conventions in [[def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference]]. A finite configuration is a finite subset $\\omega\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ with no repeated points; its number of points is $N(\\omega)$. A pair potential is an even Borel function, measurable for the Borel sigma-algebra generated by open sets ([[def-borel-sigma-algebra]]), $\\phi:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$, where even means $\\phi(-x)=\\phi(x)$. Define\n$$H_\\phi(\\omega)=\\sum_{\\{x,y\\}\\subset\\omega}\\phi(x-y),\\qquad H_\\phi(\\varnothing)=0.$$\nThe sum is over unordered two-point subsets, so every term is evaluated at a nonzero separation; if any term is $+\\infty$, the sum is $+\\infty$.\n\nThe potential is stable if some $B\\geq0$ satisfies $H_\\phi(\\omega)\\geq-BN(\\omega)$ for every finite configuration. It is superstable in the Georgii splitting convention if $\\phi=\\phi_s+\\phi_r$, where $\\phi_s$ is stable and $\\phi_r\\geq0$ everywhere and, for some $r_0,\\delta>0$, $\\phi_r(x)\\geq\\delta$ whenever $|x|_\\infty\\leq r_0$. It is lower regular if a nonnegative nonincreasing $\\psi:[0,\\infty)\\to[0,+\\infty]$ satisfies\n$$\\phi(x)\\geq-\\psi(|x|_\\infty),\\qquad \\int_0^\\infty\\psi(r)r^{d-1}\\,dr<\\infty.$$\nIt is regular if it is lower regular and, for some $R<\\infty$, $\\phi(x)\\leq\\psi(|x|_\\infty)$ whenever $|x|_\\infty\\geq R$. These are the conventions used for the interacting results on this page. A genuine divergent positive core is the separate additional condition that some nonincreasing $\\chi:(0,r_1]\\to(0,\\infty)$ satisfies $\\phi(x)\\geq\\chi(|x|_\\infty)$ for $0<|x|_\\infty\\leq r_1$ and $\\int_0^{r_1}\\chi(r)r^{d-1}dr=+\\infty$; it is not implied by superstability.\n\nFor $L>0$ put $\\Lambda_L=[0,L)^d$, $V_L=L^d$, and let $H_{L,\\mathrm{free}}$ be $H_\\phi$ for configurations restricted to $\\Lambda_L$. For sufficiently large periodic cubes, periodize $\\omega\\subset\\Lambda_L$ by $\\omega^{\\mathrm{per}}=\\{x+Lk:x\\in\\omega,\\ k\\in\\mathbb Z^d\\}$ and set\n$$H_{L,\\mathrm{per}}(\\omega)=\\frac12\\sum_{x\\in\\omega}\\sum_{y\\in\\omega^{\\mathrm{per}},\\ y\\ne x}\\phi(y-x),$$\ninterpreting the value as $+\\infty$ if a positive infinite term occurs. The far image sum is absolutely convergent for all sufficiently large $L$ by [[lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap]]; this is the image convention used below. With fixed numerical length and energy units, the canonical positional partition is\n$$Z(L,N;\\beta)=\\frac1{N!}\\int_{\\Lambda_L^N}e^{-\\beta H_\\phi(q_1,\\ldots,q_N)}dq_1\\cdots dq_N,\\quad Z(L,0;\\beta)=1,$$\nwhere $\\beta>0$, and the grand partition is $\\Xi(L;a,\\beta)=\\sum_{N\\geq0}a^NZ(L,N;\\beta)$ for numerical activity $a>0$. The normalized unit-intensity Poisson version is $Z_Q(L;z,\\beta)=E_{Q_{\\Lambda_L}}[z^{N_{\\Lambda_L}}e^{-\\beta H_{L,\\mathrm{free}}}]$; since the Poisson density contributes $e^{-V_L}$, $\\Xi(L;z,\\beta)=e^{V_L}Z_Q(L;z,\\beta)$. A physical pressure identification is not part of this mathematical definition.\n\nFor a stationary probability law $P$ on the configuration space of [[def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference]], write $\\rho(P)=E_PN_{[0,1)^d}$. When the limit exists, write\n$$\\mathcal E(P)=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}V_L^{-1}E_PH_{L,\\mathrm{free}},\\qquad I(P)=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}V_L^{-1}D(P_{\\Lambda_L}\\Vert Q_{\\Lambda_L}).$$\nThe cited results below establish existence under their stated hypotheses; this definition does not assume it for every stationary law.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-continuum-variational-pressure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC, the ultrafilter extension principle, DC, and HB. Let $\\phi$ be superstable and regular as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]], and fix $z,\\beta>0$. For stationary finite-intensity configuration laws, let $I(P)$ be relative entropy density with respect to the independent unit-intensity Poisson law, $\\rho(P)=E_PN_{[0,1)^d}$, and $\\Phi(P)$ the limiting positional energy density. The existence and lower semicontinuity of $\\Phi$ under these hypotheses are part of the proof below. For free and periodic cubes define\n$$Z_{L,z,\\beta,bc}=E_{Q_{\\Lambda_L}}\\!\\left[z^{N_{\\Lambda_L}}e^{-\\beta H_{L,bc}}\\right],\\qquad bc\\in\\{\\mathrm{free},\\mathrm{per}\\}.$$\nThen the limit $p_Q(z,\\beta)=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}L^{-d}\\log Z_{L,z,\\beta,bc}$ exists, is independent of $bc$, and\n$$p_Q(z,\\beta)=-\\min_{P\\in\\mathcal P^1_{\\rm stat}}\\{I(P)+\\beta\\Phi(P)-\\rho(P)\\log z\\}.$$\nThe minimizer set is nonempty and compact in the local-tame topology $\\tau_L$. This theorem concerns positional configurations only. If $\\Xi_{L,z,\\beta}$ is instead normalized against Lebesgue measure with $1/N!$, then $\\Xi_{L,z,\\beta}=e^{L^d}Z_{L,z,\\beta,\\mathrm{free}}$, so its logarithmic rate is $p_Q+1$.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-interval-event-ensemble-limit",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the superstable regular model and Poisson conventions of [[thm-tdmath-continuum-variational-pressure]]. Let $D\\subset[0,\\infty)$ be an interval of positive length, let $\\nu$ be the supremum of finite-entropy, finite-energy attainable densities, and set $e_0(\\rho)=\\inf\\{\\Phi(P):\\rho(P)=\\rho,\\ I(P)<\\infty\\}$. If $\\inf D<\\nu$ and $e>\\inf_{\\rho\\in D}e_0(\\rho)$, define\n$$A_L=\\{N_{\\Lambda_L}/L^d\\in D,\\ H_{L,\\mathrm{per}}/L^d\\leq e\\}.$$\nThen $A_L$ has positive Poisson probability for all sufficiently large $L$, and\n$$\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}L^{-d}\\log Q_{\\Lambda_L}(A_L) =-\\min\\{I(P):\\rho(P)\\in\\overline D,\\ \\Phi(P)\\leq e\\}.$$\nEvery $\\tau_L$ accumulation point of the conditional law $Q_{\\Lambda_L}(\\cdot\\mid A_L)$ minimizes one common functional $I+\\beta\\Phi-\\rho\\log z$ for some $\\beta\\geq0,z>0$. The periodic grand-canonical accumulation points minimize that same functional. If its minimizer is unique, both sequences converge in $\\tau_L$. The energy constraint is an upper event and $D$ is nondegenerate; this does not assert exact $N$, a shrinking shell, or a singular energy surface.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-retained-hard-zero-beta-limit",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\phi$ be superstable and regular as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]], and let $B=\\{x:\\phi(x)=+\\infty\\}$. For a finite configuration $\\omega$, call it admissible when no pair displacement belongs to $B$; for periodic boxes require this also for every periodic image pair. Define the zero-inverse-temperature-parameter continuation (the mathematical $\\beta=0$ branch) to be $z^{N(\\omega)}\\mathbf 1_{\\rm admissible}(\\omega)$, explicitly retaining the hard exclusion. Then the normalized Poisson free and periodic partition rates exist and equal\n$$-\\min_{P\\in\\mathcal K_B}\\{I(P)-\\rho(P)\\log z\\},$$\nwhere $\\mathcal K_B$ is the set of stationary laws with no forbidden pair almost surely. The minimizer set is nonempty and compact in $\\tau_L$. A singleton minimizer implies local-$\\tau_L$ convergence of both ensembles. If $B=\\varnothing$, this is the Poisson activity-$z$ model. For $B\\ne\\varnothing$, it is not an unrestricted Poisson law and does not assign a value to the undefined product $0\\cdot(+\\infty)$.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "If $\\beta>0$, the Borel superstable regular hypotheses are exactly those of [L0], and the physical conditional preparation agrees with the periodic Poisson-reference conditional law in [L2]: the factor $e^{-V_n}$ between $Q_{\\Lambda_n}$ and the unnormalized positional measure is constant on the conditioning event and cancels in normalization. Under the stated strict inequalities, [L2] gives eventual event positivity, its rate limit, and the common-minimizer accumulation statement.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "A1",
            "L0",
            "L1",
            "L2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "If $\\beta=0$, [F2] excludes every forbidden infinite-energy configuration from the finite upper-energy event, so the physical event lies in the retained-admissibility model rather than an undefined $0\\cdot(+\\infty)$ expression. [L2] gives its event rate and conditional accumulation points; [L3] identifies the corresponding retained grand variational minimizers.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "L2",
            "L3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "In either applicable branch, when the common variational minimizer set furnished by that branch is the singleton $P^\\ast$ in [A2], every local-tame accumulation point is $P^\\ast$. Compactness of the supplier's local-tame accumulation sets then gives convergence of the conditioned and corresponding grand laws for each stated observable. The conclusion remains conditional on the draft mathematical results and the selected uniqueness premise.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A2",
            "L2",
            "L3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
