    "ex-td-two-particle-hard-core-preparation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix an integer spatial dimension $d\\geq 1$, a reference length $\\ell_0>0$, and a bounded measurable region $\\Lambda\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ with dimensionless volume $V=|\\Lambda|$. Its physical region is $\\ell_0\\Lambda$. A finite positional configuration of $N$ classical point particles in $\\Lambda$ is an unordered simple set $\\omega=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_N\\}\\subset\\Lambda$; simple means that no point is repeated. Particle number $N$ is a dimensionless nonnegative integer and the physical number density corresponding to $N/V$ is $N/(\\ell_0^dV)$.\n\nA pair potential is an even measurable function $\\varphi:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$, where $\\varphi(x)$ is the interaction energy in joules at physical displacement $\\ell_0x$ and evenness means $\\varphi(x)=\\varphi(-x)$. For a labelled representative of $\\omega$, define the positional pair energy\n$$H_N(\\omega)=\\sum_{1\\leq i<j\\leq N}\\varphi(x_i-x_j),\\qquad H_0=H_1=0.$$\nIf any pair is forbidden by the potential, its energy and hence $H_N$ are $+\\infty$. This model contains no kinetic energy, internal molecular coordinates, quantum exchange terms, or external field.\n\nThe interaction is stable if there is a constant $B\\geq0$, measured in joules, such that $H_N(\\omega)\\geq-BN$ for every finite simple configuration. It has a regular integrable tail if there are $R>0$ and a nonnegative nonincreasing measurable function $\\psi:[R,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty)$, measured in joules, such that $|\\varphi(x)|\\leq\\psi(|x|)$ for $|x|\\geq R$ and\n$$\\int_R^\\infty r^{d-1}\\psi(r)\\,dr<\\infty.$$\nA stable potential with this tail condition is the stable regularly tempered class used for the free-cube results on this page. Superstability and a positive divergent core are stronger, separate hypotheses and are not implied by stability.\n\nAt temperature $T>0$, write $\\beta=(k_BT)^{-1}$ in joules inverse. The activity $z>0$ is dimensionless relative to the reference intensity $\\ell_0^{-d}$. A free-wall finite-volume model contains only positions in $\\Lambda$ and uses $H_N$ above. A periodic model uses the declared periodic cell and sums its periodic-image pair interactions with each unordered pair counted once. These are distinct boundary preparations. The limits on this page are positional configurational limits and do not by themselves include a momentum integral or a full phase-space energy shell.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt the unlabelled positional configuration measure $\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}$ obtained from labelled tuples by dividing by permutation multiplicity. Thus, for every nonnegative measurable symmetric function $f$ on $\\Lambda^N$,\n$$\\int f(\\omega)\\,\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}(d\\omega)=\\frac1{N!}\\int_{\\Lambda^N}f(\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_N\\})\\,d^{dN}x.$$\nThe reference coordinates and length scale are those of [[def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation]]. This convention treats two labelled lists that differ only by a particle permutation as the same positional state. The factor $1/N!$ is an adopted indistinguishable-particle measure convention for this classical model, not a theorem that classical trajectories or every classical description must use quotient states.\n\nFor later comparison, define the unit-intensity Poisson reference on a finite region by\n$$Q_\\Lambda(d\\omega)=e^{-|\\Lambda|}\\sum_{N=0}^{\\infty}\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}(d\\omega).$$\nIts total mass is one because $\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}$ has total mass $|\\Lambda|^N/N!$ and $e^{-|\\Lambda|}\\sum_{N\\geq0}|\\Lambda|^N/N!=1$. This is a positional reference measure in the chosen dimensionless coordinates; it supplies no empirical claim about particle trajectories.\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "post-td-equilibrium-ensemble-sampling",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "For a specified finite region and boundary preparation, take $T>0$, $\\beta=(k_BT)^{-1}$, and the positional energy in [[def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation]]. For each fixed $N$, specialize the measurable-model canonical preparation of [[post-td-canonical-sampling]] to the dimensionless positional configuration measure $\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}$; adopt the law\n$$P_{\\Lambda,N}^{\\mathrm{can}}(d\\omega)=Z_{\\Lambda,N}^{-1}e^{-\\beta H_N(\\omega)}\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}(d\\omega),\\qquad Z_{\\Lambda,N}=\\int e^{-\\beta H_N}\\,d\\mu_{\\Lambda,N},$$\nwhenever $0<Z_{\\Lambda,N}<\\infty$. Separately adopt the grand-canonical law at activity $z>0$ to have weights $z^{N(\\omega)}e^{-\\beta H_{N(\\omega)}(\\omega)}$ relative to the same indistinguishable positional measures; its normalizer is $\\Xi_\\Lambda(z,\\beta)=\\sum_{N\\geq0}z^NZ_{\\Lambda,N}$ when finite. Here $z$ is dimensionless relative to the reference intensity in [[post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure]], and $\\alpha=\\log z$.\n\nFor the controlled positional shell of [[def-td-controlled-fixed-n-energy-shell]], adopt the conditional measure $M_n$ only when its shell has positive finite reference measure. These are probability-law preparations used to define the model. They are not derived from Hamiltonian trajectories, and they do not assert that a laboratory apparatus reaches any of them. The inverse-temperature-zero hard-core continuation is governed separately by [[def-td-retained-hard-core-zero-beta-preparation]].\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The allowed labelled pairs form two disjoint triangles, $x_2\\geq x_1+a$ and $x_1\\geq x_2+a$. The first has area $\\int_0^{L-a}(L-a-x_1)\\,dx_1=(L-a)^2/2$, and the second has the same area by exchanging coordinates.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Their total allowed area is $(L-a)^2$. On it $e^{-\\beta H_2}=1$; on the excluded band it is zero because $H_2=+\\infty$. Multiplying the allowed area by the measure factor $1/2$ from [A1] gives $Z_{\\Lambda,2}=(L-a)^2/2$, as asserted.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
