    "ex-td-forbidden-pair-beta-zero": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-td-retained-hard-core-zero-beta-preparation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For the pair potential $\\varphi$ of [[def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation]], let $B=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^d:\\varphi(x)=+\\infty\\}$. A finite configuration is hard-admissible if every pair displacement avoids $B$. For $z>0$, define the retained-admissibility zero-inverse-temperature weight of $\\omega$ to be\n$$w_0(\\omega)=z^{N(\\omega)}\\mathbf 1_{\\{H_{N(\\omega)}(\\omega)<\\infty\\}}.$$\nIts finite-volume partition sum, when finite, is $\\Xi_{\\Lambda}^{\\mathrm{ret},0}(z)=\\sum_{N\\geq0}\\int w_0(\\omega)\\,\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}(d\\omega)$, using [[post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure]]. This definition sets the weight of a forbidden configuration to zero; it does not assign a value to the undefined product $0\\cdot(+\\infty)$. When $B=\\varnothing$ the measure is the unrestricted Poisson activity model; for a nonempty hard core it is generally not Poisson. The convention is explicit model data and carries no statement about an arbitrary zero-width energy shell.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt the unlabelled positional configuration measure $\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}$ obtained from labelled tuples by dividing by permutation multiplicity. Thus, for every nonnegative measurable symmetric function $f$ on $\\Lambda^N$,\n$$\\int f(\\omega)\\,\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}(d\\omega)=\\frac1{N!}\\int_{\\Lambda^N}f(\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_N\\})\\,d^{dN}x.$$\nThe reference coordinates and length scale are those of [[def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation]]. This convention treats two labelled lists that differ only by a particle permutation as the same positional state. The factor $1/N!$ is an adopted indistinguishable-particle measure convention for this classical model, not a theorem that classical trajectories or every classical description must use quotient states.\n\nFor later comparison, define the unit-intensity Poisson reference on a finite region by\n$$Q_\\Lambda(d\\omega)=e^{-|\\Lambda|}\\sum_{N=0}^{\\infty}\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}(d\\omega).$$\nIts total mass is one because $\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}$ has total mass $|\\Lambda|^N/N!$ and $e^{-|\\Lambda|}\\sum_{N\\geq0}|\\Lambda|^N/N!=1$. This is a positional reference measure in the chosen dimensionless coordinates; it supplies no empirical claim about particle trajectories.\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F1] and [A1], every pair in the open band $|x_1-x_2|<a$ has zero retained weight, while every admissible pair has weight $z^2$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "A1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The band has positive Lebesgue area $2aL-a^2>0$, so the unrestricted Poisson activity measure assigns it positive mass, whereas the retained law assigns it zero mass. Therefore the two probability laws differ.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
