    "ex-td-volume-sized-external-field": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix an integer spatial dimension $d\\geq 1$, a reference length $\\ell_0>0$, and a bounded measurable region $\\Lambda\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ with dimensionless volume $V=|\\Lambda|$. Its physical region is $\\ell_0\\Lambda$. A finite positional configuration of $N$ classical point particles in $\\Lambda$ is an unordered simple set $\\omega=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_N\\}\\subset\\Lambda$; simple means that no point is repeated. Particle number $N$ is a dimensionless nonnegative integer and the physical number density corresponding to $N/V$ is $N/(\\ell_0^dV)$.\n\nA pair potential is an even measurable function $\\varphi:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$, where $\\varphi(x)$ is the interaction energy in joules at physical displacement $\\ell_0x$ and evenness means $\\varphi(x)=\\varphi(-x)$. For a labelled representative of $\\omega$, define the positional pair energy\n$$H_N(\\omega)=\\sum_{1\\leq i<j\\leq N}\\varphi(x_i-x_j),\\qquad H_0=H_1=0.$$\nIf any pair is forbidden by the potential, its energy and hence $H_N$ are $+\\infty$. This model contains no kinetic energy, internal molecular coordinates, quantum exchange terms, or external field.\n\nThe interaction is stable if there is a constant $B\\geq0$, measured in joules, such that $H_N(\\omega)\\geq-BN$ for every finite simple configuration. It has a regular integrable tail if there are $R>0$ and a nonnegative nonincreasing measurable function $\\psi:[R,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty)$, measured in joules, such that $|\\varphi(x)|\\leq\\psi(|x|)$ for $|x|\\geq R$ and\n$$\\int_R^\\infty r^{d-1}\\psi(r)\\,dr<\\infty.$$\nA stable potential with this tail condition is the stable regularly tempered class used for the free-cube results on this page. Superstability and a positive divergent core are stronger, separate hypotheses and are not implied by stability.\n\nAt temperature $T>0$, write $\\beta=(k_BT)^{-1}$ in joules inverse. The activity $z>0$ is dimensionless relative to the reference intensity $\\ell_0^{-d}$. A free-wall finite-volume model contains only positions in $\\Lambda$ and uses $H_N$ above. A periodic model uses the declared periodic cell and sums its periodic-image pair interactions with each unordered pair counted once. These are distinct boundary preparations. The limits on this page are positional configurational limits and do not by themselves include a momentum integral or a full phase-space energy shell.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-td-equilibrium-ensemble-sampling",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "For a specified finite region and boundary preparation, take $T>0$, $\\beta=(k_BT)^{-1}$, and the positional energy in [[def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation]]. For each fixed $N$, specialize the measurable-model canonical preparation of [[post-td-canonical-sampling]] to the dimensionless positional configuration measure $\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}$; adopt the law\n$$P_{\\Lambda,N}^{\\mathrm{can}}(d\\omega)=Z_{\\Lambda,N}^{-1}e^{-\\beta H_N(\\omega)}\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}(d\\omega),\\qquad Z_{\\Lambda,N}=\\int e^{-\\beta H_N}\\,d\\mu_{\\Lambda,N},$$\nwhenever $0<Z_{\\Lambda,N}<\\infty$. Separately adopt the grand-canonical law at activity $z>0$ to have weights $z^{N(\\omega)}e^{-\\beta H_{N(\\omega)}(\\omega)}$ relative to the same indistinguishable positional measures; its normalizer is $\\Xi_\\Lambda(z,\\beta)=\\sum_{N\\geq0}z^NZ_{\\Lambda,N}$ when finite. Here $z$ is dimensionless relative to the reference intensity in [[post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure]], and $\\alpha=\\log z$.\n\nFor the controlled positional shell of [[def-td-controlled-fixed-n-energy-shell]], adopt the conditional measure $M_n$ only when its shell has positive finite reference measure. These are probability-law preparations used to define the model. They are not derived from Hamiltonian trajectories, and they do not assert that a laboratory apparatus reaches any of them. The inverse-temperature-zero hard-core continuation is governed separately by [[def-td-retained-hard-core-zero-beta-preparation]].\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-td-admissible-van-hove-and-tempered-exterior",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the dimensionless coordinates, simple configurations, and pair potential of [[def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation]]. A sequence $(\\Lambda_k)_{k\\geq1}$ of bounded measurable regions is van Hove if $|\\Lambda_k|\\to\\infty$ and, for every fixed $s>0$,\n$$\\frac{|\\{x\\in\\Lambda_k:\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\partial\\Lambda_k)\\leq s\\}|}{|\\Lambda_k|}\\longrightarrow0.$$\nThis is an explicit condition on the boundary-layer volume; increasing volume alone does not imply it. A free wall permits particles only in $\\Lambda_k$ and includes no exterior particles. A periodic wall uses the specified periodic cell and its image convention.\n\nFor the deterministic exterior class, let $C=[-1/2,1/2)^d$ and $\\Lambda_n=[-n-1/2,n+1/2)^d$, with $V_n=|\\Lambda_n|$. For a locally finite exterior configuration $\\zeta\\subset\\mathbb R^d$, write $N_{C+i}(\\zeta)=|\\zeta\\cap(C+i)|$. Given $t<\\infty$, define\n$$\\Omega(t)=\\left\\{\\zeta:\\sum_{i\\in\\Lambda_n\\cap\\mathbb Z^d}N_{C+i}(\\zeta)^2\\leq tV_n\\ \\text{for every integer }n\\geq0\\right\\}.$$\nFor an interior configuration $\\omega\\subset\\Lambda_n$, its exterior interaction is\n$$W_n(\\omega,\\zeta)=\\sum_{x\\in\\omega}\\sum_{y\\in\\zeta\\setminus\\Lambda_n}\\varphi(x-y),$$\ninterpreted as an extended-real sum. A positive infinite value has zero Boltzmann weight. The regular tail controls distant terms; the class $\\Omega(t)$ is a fixed uniform bound on exterior cell counts, not permission for an arbitrary volume-filling external potential. The van Hove and uniformly tempered exterior conditions are separate boundary regimes.\n",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "pthm-td-qualified-van-hove-and-exterior-limit",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC, as required by the current mathematical definition and its shape suppliers. Let $d\\geq2$, $E_0=1\\,\\mathrm J$, $\\widehat\\varphi=\\varphi/E_0$, and assume $\\widehat\\varphi$ is Borel measurable and superstable and regular as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]]. Use $\\widehat H=H/E_0$ and $\\widehat\\beta=E_0(k_BT)^{-1}>0$ in the mathematical rates. Let $(\\Lambda_k)$ be a van Hove sequence as in [[def-td-admissible-van-hove-and-tempered-exterior]]. First, for free walls, the positional grand pressure along $\\Lambda_k$ converges to the free-cube pressure of [[pthm-td-stable-tempered-free-cube-grand-pressure]]. For any positive interior attainable density $\\rho$ and exact integers $N_k$ with $N_k/|\\Lambda_k|\\to\\rho$, the exact-$N_k$ free-wall canonical log-partition rate converges to the same cube canonical rate. These two shape statements require superstability and regularity; stability alone is not asserted to suffice for arbitrary van Hove shapes.\n\nSecond, strengthen the potential hypothesis: for some $r_0>0$, $\\widehat\\varphi(x)\\geq\\widehat\\chi(\\|x\\|_\\infty)>0$ whenever $0<\\|x\\|_\\infty\\leq r_0$, where $\\widehat\\chi$ is nonincreasing and $\\int_0^{r_0}\\widehat\\chi(r)r^{d-1}\\,dr=+\\infty$. For each fixed $t<\\infty$, the free-wall grand pressure in $\\Lambda_n$ with exterior $\\zeta\\in\\Omega(t)$ then converges uniformly in $\\zeta$ to the same free-cube pressure. The exterior interaction is the deterministic positional term of [[def-td-admissible-van-hove-and-tempered-exterior]], not a volume-filling external field. This genuine divergent-core condition is the exact stronger hypothesis of the current exterior supplier; it is not inferred from superstability or replaced by a weaker source condition.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Since $H_N=0$, the finite-volume canonical positional integral is $Z_{V,N}=V^N/N!$. Summing the adopted grand weights gives $\\Xi_V=\\sum_{N\\geq0}(zV)^N/N!=e^{zV}$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Adding $cN$ to the energy multiplies the $N$-particle weight by $e^{-\\beta cN}$, so $\\Xi_V^{(c)}=\\sum_{N\\geq0}(ze^{-\\beta c}V)^N/N!=e^{ze^{-\\beta c}V}$. For $c\\neq0$ this has a different log-partition rate.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
